WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.692
Welcome to GitHub Manager.

00:00:02.692 --> 00:00:07.770
This walkthrough shows the complete local workflow, one step at a time.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.731
First, connect and sync your GitHub account.

00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:18.770
Then inspect repositories, build and approve a taxonomy, review AI suggestions, and
organize your work manually or with rules.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.155
We finish with persistence and safe reset tests.

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:25.117
The screen is recorded at full HD.

00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:30.617
You signed in before recording, so no password or authorization screen is included.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.662
Start in My GitHub workspace.

00:00:34.662 --> 00:00:38.707
Click Sync GitHub and wait for the run to finish.

00:00:38.707 --> 00:00:42.751
Synchronization imports repository relationships and metadata independently of AI
organization.

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:46.796
It does not send repository content to a language model.

00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:53.267
The source coverage section explains what GitHub returned and whether a source is
partial or unavailable.

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.502
Private repository names are masked in this recording.

00:00:56.502 --> 00:01:02.165
Their presence can still be checked locally without disclosing their names in the
video.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.203
My Repos, Favorites and Access candidates answer different questions.

00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:12.206
My Repos contains your eligible or explicitly included work.

00:01:12.206 --> 00:01:15.319
Favorites reflects observed star and watch relationships.

00:01:15.319 --> 00:01:18.877
Access candidates shows repositories the account can access.

00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:24.214
Use search and the language or visibility filters to narrow the list.

00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:26.882
Open details for metadata and memberships.

00:01:26.882 --> 00:01:30.440
Private details show that AI evidence is blocked.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:36.222
A repository does not become your project just because it is publicly readable.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.224
Latest Work shows your own dated contributions.

00:01:41.224 --> 00:01:45.488
Change the window between seven, fourteen, thirty and ninety days.

00:01:45.488 --> 00:01:52.735
This view uses the signed-in numeric GitHub identity and contribution timestamps,
rather than another person's push date.

00:01:52.735 --> 00:01:56.999
Empty results are valid when no supported activity was observed.

00:01:56.999 --> 00:01:59.983
Check the activity coverage message as well.

00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:08.510
This is a bounded contribution view, so partial or unavailable GitHub coverage must
not be mistaken for a complete history.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:20.793
For the remaining feature and reset tests, we switch to a disposable local
application database and an explicitly labeled test identity.

00:02:20.793 --> 00:02:28.131
The user interface, authentication controls, persistence, organization services and
reset logic are the actual application.

00:02:28.131 --> 00:02:33.512
The GitHub account and inventory in this isolated segment are fixtures.

00:02:33.512 --> 00:02:40.361
New AI classification uses the real configured OpenAI provider; cached taxonomy is
labeled reused.

00:02:40.361 --> 00:02:46.231
This separation protects the working GitHub workspace while we exercise destructive
controls.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.422
Choose Public plus synthetic demo in the workspace selector, then Load demo samples.

00:02:53.422 --> 00:02:59.401
The collection has one approved public FastAPI sample and two clearly labeled
synthetic Orion repositories.

00:02:59.401 --> 00:03:02.590
It is separate from My Repos and Favorites.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:08.170
Opening those relationship views does not pretend the samples belong to the signed-
in account.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.960
Return to Demo samples for the tutorial.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.736
These three examples are small enough to understand and keep model use within the
approved request limit.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.026
Open AI Organizer and run Extract evidence.

00:03:23.026 --> 00:03:28.625
Extraction builds a versioned local evidence profile without a language model call.

00:03:28.625 --> 00:03:33.291
Back in repository details, inspect the phrases and supporting sections.

00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:38.424
These are the compact inputs used by taxonomy discovery and classification.

00:03:38.424 --> 00:03:42.157
The application default uses RAKE and TF IDF.

00:03:42.157 --> 00:03:45.889
Earlier BM25 and YAKE benchmarking remains available separately.

00:03:45.889 --> 00:03:52.422
Missing source content is reported; private README content is excluded from the
model path.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:04:00.209
In AI Organizer, keep Reuse validated cached AI selected and click Discover
taxonomy.

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:06.865
A successful matching cache returns the earlier validated real model result without
new OpenAI requests.

00:04:06.865 --> 00:04:09.527
The draft is clearly labeled reused.

00:04:09.527 --> 00:04:15.296
A cache miss produces an explicit error and does not silently spend money.

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:20.177
Discovery proposes vocabulary; it does not publish concepts or assign repositories.

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:25.945
The review step is where you decide which concepts belong in your workspace.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.245
Open Review draft.

00:04:29.245 --> 00:04:39.524
A domain is a broad area, a project groups related repositories, a topic describes
purpose, and a tag captures a useful technology or attribute.

00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:43.378
Edit a description or alias and save the draft.

00:04:43.378 --> 00:04:46.804
You can exclude unwanted proposals with their checkboxes.

00:04:46.804 --> 00:04:48.946
Projects need a parent domain.

00:04:48.946 --> 00:04:52.800
Equivalent names should become aliases of one canonical concept.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.940
Saving a draft preserves your edits while leaving the published registry unchanged.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.635
Reopen the saved draft and click Approve and publish taxonomy.

00:05:04.635 --> 00:05:11.181
Publication is an explicit human decision and changes the canonical taxonomy in one
revision.

00:05:11.181 --> 00:05:14.921
The sidebar now exposes the accepted project structure.

00:05:14.921 --> 00:05:19.597
Stable concept identifiers let names change later without breaking memberships.

00:05:19.597 --> 00:05:24.272
Publication alone does not classify repositories or accept AI assignments.

00:05:24.272 --> 00:05:30.350
You can inspect the four concept types in Content settings before starting
classification.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.587
Select the three sample repositories and open AI Organizer.

00:05:36.587 --> 00:05:39.405
Choose Live AI, then Full classification.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:46.919
This is fresh model inference through the application's LangChain layer, not fixture
output or cached replay.

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:50.676
Watch the persisted progress and final run outcomes.

00:05:50.676 --> 00:05:57.720
Three classifications normally require six HTTP requests because each generation has
a token count request.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.887
The existing global request and five dollar spending caps remain enforced.

00:06:02.887 --> 00:06:05.704
No private repository content is included.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:11.465
Review each suggestion's concept, reason and supporting evidence.

00:06:11.465 --> 00:06:15.677
Accept a useful project suggestion and reject another suggestion.

00:06:15.677 --> 00:06:19.890
Acceptance turns the mapping into a protected USER decision.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:23.634
Rejection records a protected decision against automatic recreation.

00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:33.932
Pending suggestions do not count as accepted project organization, and a topic or
tag alone does not remove a repository from Uncategorized.

00:06:33.932 --> 00:06:39.548
Model confidence is not calibrated, so evidence and your judgment remain essential.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.326
Now organize without AI.

00:06:43.326 --> 00:06:51.274
Create a second project, select the Orion repositories, choose that project in the
bulk toolbar and add membership.

00:06:51.274 --> 00:06:56.132
Drag one repository onto another project to add an additional membership.

00:06:56.132 --> 00:06:59.664
Open its details and remove only one project.

00:06:59.664 --> 00:07:01.872
The other membership must remain.

00:07:01.872 --> 00:07:05.404
Manual controls support multiple projects, topics and tags.

00:07:05.404 --> 00:07:13.794
This test also shows that organization remains usable when model access is
unavailable or the request allowance is exhausted.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.301
Content settings is where you maintain the vocabulary.

00:07:19.301 --> 00:07:25.866
Create a temporary domain, then create a project and select that domain as its
parent.

00:07:25.866 --> 00:07:28.930
Add a clear description and optional aliases.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:34.619
The domain appears in project navigation, while the project can receive repository
memberships.

00:07:34.619 --> 00:07:38.996
Domains are derived through project membership in this local demo.

00:07:38.996 --> 00:07:45.123
Create only concepts that have a distinct purpose; excessive duplicates make
classification less useful.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.418
Edit the temporary project, rename it, and add an alias.

00:07:51.418 --> 00:07:56.527
Its identifier stays the same, so existing relationships can survive a rename.

00:07:56.527 --> 00:07:59.934
Move it to another parent domain if needed.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:05.043
Next, try creating another project with a conflicting normalized name or alias.

00:08:05.043 --> 00:08:08.449
The server rejects the conflict clearly and atomically.

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:11.004
Close the invalid form without saving.

00:08:11.004 --> 00:08:17.817
This demonstrates that canonical naming is enforced by the backend rather than only
by browser controls.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.304
Create a temporary equivalent project and merge it into the retained project.

00:08:25.304 --> 00:08:29.857
The target keeps its identity; memberships and aliases are reconciled.

00:08:29.857 --> 00:08:32.133
Next archive the temporary domain.

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:38.052
The confirmation explains that surviving projects move to protected Unassigned
rather than disappearing.

00:08:38.052 --> 00:08:44.882
Archiving a concept removes its dependent active memberships while retaining name
reservations against accidental recreation.

00:08:44.882 --> 00:08:52.622
Inspect the result after each operation instead of assuming an archive and a merge
have identical effects.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.740
Create an organization rule with a simple supported metadata condition.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:03.251
Here we match the Python language and assign a temporary tag.

00:09:03.251 --> 00:09:06.941
Click Preview matches first and inspect the repository count.

00:09:06.941 --> 00:09:08.991
Only then approve the rule.

00:09:08.991 --> 00:09:11.861
Preview does not apply assignments by itself.

00:09:11.861 --> 00:09:17.191
Rules can use local accessible private metadata without sending it to a model.

00:09:17.191 --> 00:09:21.292
They remain subordinate to protected USER mappings and rejection decisions.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.292
Choose Rules only in AI Organizer.

00:09:26.292 --> 00:09:29.265
Run Reorganize incremental, then Full classification.

00:09:29.265 --> 00:09:36.200
Both controls operate against the current taxonomy and record processed, failed and
per-repository outcomes.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:44.127
In this local adapter, incremental reconciliation currently visits the selected
cohort; production delta indexing is deferred.

00:09:44.127 --> 00:09:47.595
Inspect Run history for the actual result.

00:09:47.595 --> 00:09:54.035
The rule-created tag is visible in details, while existing manual mappings remain
intact.

00:09:54.035 --> 00:09:57.007
These runs make no OpenAI calls.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.199
Switch back to Reuse validated cached AI and choose Rediscover taxonomy.

00:10:04.199 --> 00:10:06.544
It creates another reviewable draft.

00:10:06.544 --> 00:10:12.171
It does not silently replace your published names, projects or manual memberships.

00:10:12.171 --> 00:10:18.737
Open the proposal, inspect its reused provenance, then reject the draft for this
test.

00:10:18.737 --> 00:10:21.551
The active taxonomy must remain unchanged.

00:10:21.551 --> 00:10:29.993
Use rediscovery when the vocabulary itself needs reconsideration; use classification
when you want assignments against the current vocabulary.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.413
Automatic organization is optional and off by default.

00:10:35.413 --> 00:10:40.532
Enable it, then create two distinct projects to meet the significant-change
threshold.

00:10:40.532 --> 00:10:47.358
The real scheduler waits for ninety seconds without further edits and respects a
thirty minute cooldown.

00:10:47.358 --> 00:10:53.757
Most of that wait is cut from this recording; the test uses the real interval.

00:10:53.757 --> 00:10:59.730
This scheduler test uses explicitly labeled fixture AI to conserve the remaining
request allowance.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:06.982
Inspect the AUTO run, confirm protected decisions remain, then disable automatic
organization before further editing or resets.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.683
Reload the browser and verify the sample inventory and organization remain.

00:11:13.683 --> 00:11:20.068
Sign out, sign back into the isolated test account, and select the sample collection
again.

00:11:20.068 --> 00:11:23.048
Next restart only the isolated recording backend.

00:11:23.048 --> 00:11:27.304
The database remains, but the in-memory login session does not.

00:11:27.304 --> 00:11:33.264
Sign in again and verify the same taxonomy and manual memberships are still present.

00:11:33.264 --> 00:11:37.095
Interrupted inference must be disclosed rather than silently repeated.

00:11:37.095 --> 00:11:41.352
Your normal application backend is not restarted by this test.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.164
Open Latest Work in the sample collection.

00:11:46.164 --> 00:11:49.769
Synthetic Orion activity is explicitly labeled as a fixture.

00:11:49.769 --> 00:11:52.974
Change the date window and inspect the results.

00:11:52.974 --> 00:12:00.185
This is a repeatable test of the activity interface and filtering, not evidence of a
real GitHub contribution.

00:12:00.185 --> 00:12:04.190
The earlier live GitHub segment showed the actual account observation.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:11.401
Keeping those sources separate makes the walkthrough useful without inventing real
activity when the upstream account has none.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:18.065
Open Preview reset and leave user taxonomy and rules unselected.

00:12:18.065 --> 00:12:22.691
Select the desired AI state and cached rediscovery after cleanup.

00:12:22.691 --> 00:12:25.004
Inspect the server-verified impact counts.

00:12:25.004 --> 00:12:31.942
A trailing space after DELETE is not accepted; enter the exact word to enable
confirmation.

00:12:31.942 --> 00:12:34.717
After reset, wait for Cleanup verified.

00:12:34.717 --> 00:12:42.581
The accepted manual project mapping and protected rejection must survive, along with
repository metadata and published taxonomy.

00:12:42.581 --> 00:12:45.818
Cached restart creates a new proposal only.

00:12:45.818 --> 00:12:50.444
The historical readiness reports and API ledger are outside reset.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:59.334
For a partial reset, clear the AI checkboxes and select only Domains under user
taxonomy.

00:12:59.334 --> 00:13:03.903
Preview the exact impact and confirm in the isolated collection.

00:13:03.903 --> 00:13:06.645
Surviving projects move to protected Unassigned.

00:13:06.645 --> 00:13:14.413
The selected domain content is removed, while unrelated topics, tags, repository
metadata and surviving project identities remain.

00:13:14.413 --> 00:13:23.094
This illustrates why you should preview each reset scope rather than treating every
reset as a complete workspace deletion.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:30.961
Finally select all user taxonomy kinds, organization rules and the desired AI state.

00:13:30.961 --> 00:13:36.353
Review a fresh impact preview, type DELETE and wait for verified cleanup.

00:13:36.353 --> 00:13:42.643
Full taxonomy reset starts at revision zero in a new epoch; repository metadata
remains.

00:13:42.643 --> 00:13:47.585
Request cached discovery to rebuild a reviewable draft, then publish it.

00:13:47.585 --> 00:13:53.426
New concepts have new identifiers, so old classification results cannot be blindly
replayed.

00:13:53.426 --> 00:13:59.717
Use manual assignments or rules to restore a rehearsal state without additional
model calls.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:08.358
The walkthrough has exercised the local application's main controls with assertions
and saved outcomes.

00:14:08.358 --> 00:14:15.470
The accompanying Markdown guide lists chapter times, exact test coverage, provider
provenance and any limitations.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:22.582
Earlier backend and browser regression results, real AI readiness evidence and
preservation reports remain intact.

00:14:22.582 --> 00:14:29.694
This is the local MVP: cloud deployment, distributed workers and production scale
validation remain deferred.

00:14:29.694 --> 00:14:40.599
To repeat the workflow, start with sync, use the sample collection for organization,
review every AI decision, and leave reset tests until last.
